                <pb facs="0295" n="195" sameAs="frus:frus1934v02/pg195"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">651.113/182: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Marriner</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1934-06-28T14:00:00+01:00">June 28, 1934—2
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received June 28—1:15 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>482. Your 443, June 19th. If the Supreme Court of the United States upholds the
                    decisions of the two courts regarding paragraph A of section 601 would not
                    France be exempt from the excise tax of 10 cents per 100 pounds on coal because
                    the United States, as long as the <hi rendition="#italic">modus vivendi</hi> of
                    1927 continues, is obliged to grant most-favored-nation treatment to France?</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Marriner</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
